#!/bin/sh
# Only emit context. The current Codex host owns authentication and usage data.
# No interpreter, CLI subprocess, account-file access, or network call here.
printf '%s\n' 'Overflow usage policy:
At the start of a new substantive user turn, use the host-native get_usage_limits tool once if available. Use the main Codex bucket from rateLimitsByLimitId.codex, falling back to rateLimits only when it is the main Codex bucket. For each available primary/secondary window, remaining = max(0, min(100, 100 - usedPercent)); the lowest remaining window is the limit. Automatic delegation applies only when that remaining value is strictly below 10 percent. Exactly 10 percent is not below the threshold. Never infer the running model from usage buckets.
When below 10 percent, use the Overflow work skill to send one self-contained order, including necessary input files, and keep this task as coordinator. Respect explicit instructions to keep work local. Do not delegate greetings, status checks, result collection, setup questions, /earn, or any work already claimed through Overflow. An earning task must complete or fail its own order, never delegate it again. /work remains available at any allowance level.
If the native usage tool is missing or cannot read a numeric main-bucket limit, keep working normally; do not install a runtime, launch a Codex CLI, read account files, or guess usage. Mention the setup limitation once when relevant to automatic delegation. This policy adds no idle polling and does not interrupt an already-running turn.
When Overflow is connected, call overflow_touch once in this user turn to record recent activity; skip it if another Overflow call already recorded this turn. Keep successful setup/activity checks quiet. Do not send conversation text or Codex allowance to the presence endpoint.'
